<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>In 1892 Elizabeth Cady Stanton was ending her term as the first president of the National American Woman Suffrage Association, formed in 1890 when the two wings of the suffrage movement merged. She gave the address that became known as The Solitude of Self three times in as many days: twice to committees of Congress, among them the Committee of the Judiciary, and once as her last address to the association.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>And yet, there is a solitude, which each and every one of us has always carried with him, more inaccessible than the ice-cold mountains, more profound than the midnight sea; the solitude of self.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">From the address to the Committee of the Judiciary, as transcribed on Wikisource; the full text is not printed here, as the transcription names no edition and has slips.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“And yet, there is a solitude, which each and every one of us has always carried with him, more inaccessible than the ice-cold mountains, more profound than the midnight sea; the solitude of self.” — Elizabeth Cady Stanton, Elizabeth Cady Stanton: The Solitude of Self" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-stanton-solitude-of-self.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>At the 1892 convention Stanton declined re-election and Susan B. Anthony became president. Stanton considered the speech her best. She grew estranged from the suffrage movement after the association voted in 1896 to distance itself from her Woman's Bible, and died in New York City in October 1902, eighteen years before the Nineteenth Amendment gave American women the vote.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution">Nineteenth Amendment</a></div></div></section>
